// @run
// @log 2.5
// @log 3.5
// @log 2
// THE i32-KEYED MAP AS THE ELEMENT OF A LIST-TYPED INLINE-SHAPE FIELD — the position
// whose ONLY collector is `ensureRefElem`'s map-element arm, alone in a file because a
// module-wide flag cannot be pinned beside anything that could set it.
//
// `{m: {[i32]: f64}[]}` is ONE `TypeRef` name, so its map reaches NO annotation arm: the
// bare-map arm never sees it (the name is a shape, not a map), and the field-table
// re-derivation's map-FIELD arm never sees it either (the field's code is 5, a ref list,
// not 19). The one thing that sees it is the ref-list ELEMENT intern, and its `k == 3`
// arm is what forces `mUsed` / `lUsed` / `aUsed`, the element map's mv VALUE slot and —
// the half this file pins — `mI32Used`.
//
// `mI32Used` gates the i32-keyed map STRUCT, the `__map_probe_i32__` helper and the
// per-function 5-slot i32-KEY scratch frame (`mapI32Base`). Unset, the frame is not
// reserved, so `mapI32Base` lands on the NEXT frame's base and the store's key temporary
// resolves to the same local index as the map-REF temporary: `type mismatch: expected
// (ref $type), found i32` at `vl check` rc 0, with `call 4294967295` (the -1 probe-helper
// index) beside it.
//
// NOTHING ELSE IN THIS FILE MAY SPELL AN i32 KEY, and the covering sources are more
// numerous than they look:
//   · a bare `const m: {[i32]: V} = Map()` binding is a bare-map `TypeRef`;
//   · a DECLARED `type S = {m: {[i32]: V}[]}` spells its FIELD as its own `TypeRef`,
//     which IS a map-array annotation — that spelling passes with the fix reverted, and
//     is why this residue survived the position that looks identical to it;
//   · a bare `{[i32]: V}[]` annotation anywhere is one too.
// A `[Map()]` LITERAL does NOT cover it here, though it covers a bare `{[i32]: V}[]`
// binding: the constructor arm reads the checker's key type off a `Map()` whose
// annotation it can find, and inside an object literal's array literal it cannot.
//
// The VALUE is `f64` — an mv-SLOT value, which is the SILENT half of the failure. The
// mono-valued twin (`{[i32]: i32}`) fails LOUDLY at the same site and is pinned in
// `i32-keyed-list-field-mono-value.vl`; keeping the two apart is what makes each a pin
// on its own class rather than on whichever fires first.

const s: {m: {[i32]: f64}[]} = { m: [Map()] }
s.m[0][1] = 2.5
s.m[0][2] = 3.5
print(s.m[0][1] ?? 0.0) // 2.5
print(s.m[0][2] ?? 0.0) // 3.5
print(s.m[0].size)      // 2
